From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 18 Aug 2026 01:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 29/29] exec: put every alert on the debug channel, not only the
 ones nobody can read

Alert() has two exits and only one of them reaches serial.

When Intuition is up, Exec_UserAlert() draws the "Software Failure!" requester
and the machine keeps running. Everything that requester knows -- the alert
code, the task, the PC, the register dump -- then exists only as pixels. On a
headless boot it is lost outright; on a real machine it has to be photographed
and typed back in, which is how the reports in ISSUE-0037 arrived, one register
at a time and with the interesting fields missing.

Exec_SystemAlert() does log, but it is reached only when the requester *cannot*
be drawn -- precisely the case where the machine is least able to explain
itself.

So log first, then display. Alert_DisplayKrnAlert() already builds exactly the
text Exec_SystemAlert() would, and hands it to KrnDisplayAlert(), whose generic
implementation (rom/kernel/_displayalert.c) prints the framed text through
krnPutC() and returns -- it does not halt, does not reboot, and does not need
Intuition. On a target with no debug channel the whole thing costs one
formatting pass into a buffer that already exists in SysBase.
---
--- a/rom/exec/alert.c
+++ b/rom/exec/alert.c
@@ -15,6 +15,10 @@
 #include "exec_util.h"
 #include "etask.h"
 
+/* Defined in systemalert.c, which declares it locally rather than in a header. */
+void Alert_DisplayKrnAlert(struct Task *task, ULONG alertNum, APTR location, APTR stack, UBYTE type, APTR data,
+        struct ExecBase *SysBase);
+
 /*****************************************************************************
 
     NAME */
@@ -141,6 +145,24 @@
     }
 
     /*
+     * Put the alert on the debug channel before anything is displayed.
+     *
+     * Alert() has two exits and only one of them says anything on serial. When
+     * Intuition is up, Exec_UserAlert() draws the requester and the machine
+     * carries on, so everything the requester knows -- the alert code, the task,
+     * the PC, the register dump -- exists only on a screen, and on a headless or
+     * photographed boot it is lost. Exec_SystemAlert() is reached only when the
+     * requester cannot be drawn, which is exactly when the machine is in no
+     * state to be asked questions.
+     *
+     * So log first, then display. Alert_DisplayKrnAlert() builds the same text
+     * Exec_SystemAlert() would and hands it to KrnDisplayAlert(), whose generic
+     * implementation prints it and returns -- no halt, no reboot, no dependency
+     * on Intuition being alive.
+     */
+    Alert_DisplayKrnAlert(task, alertNum, location, stack, type, data, SysBase);
+
+    /*
      * If we are running in user mode we should first try to report a problem
      * using Intuition display.
      */
